+**The field.** A rule may carry `command`: an array of 1 to 8 words. The first is a command name, `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9._:=+-]{0,63}$` (lowercase, as the matcher folds). A later word is either such a word or a flag, `^--?[a-z0-9][a-z0-9._:=+-]{0,61}$` (lane W's addition: the plan's `git push --force` needs one). `tool` must then be `bash` (a shell tool of step 7b's list maps to it, case 135), and the rule has no `descriptionSha256`. A rule with `command` and any other tool, or with a pin, fails the schema and every matcher. A flag as the first word is refused by the three matchers; the schema cannot say "the first item only" under the strict tuple rule its tests run with. `fixtures/policy/invalid.json` holds each refused shape.
 
-**How it matches.** Only shell tools. The folded input (step 7a's text) is split into simple commands at `;`, `&&`, `||`, `|`, `&` and line breaks; each command's words are the text split on spaces, a leading `sudo`, `env`, `command`, `nohup`, `time`, `nice` and `timeout <n>` dropped (lane X's wrapper list). A command matches when its first words equal `command`'s words in order (`["gh", "pr", "create"]` matches `gh pr create --fill`; `["git", "push"]` matches `git push --force origin x`).
+**A command rule is never a rule for the whole tool.** It takes no part in steps 3 to 5 or 3a: a `bash` rule with `command` neither allows nor denies the shell. The writers of an agent's own rules (`derm connect <agent> --rules`, Gemini's printout) leave command rules to the gate and say so: as the agent's own deny one would take the whole shell away.
 
-**What it may do.** A `deny` or `ask` command rule narrows, like step 3a: any matching command makes the call `deny` (at once) or turns an `allow` into `ask`, reason `rule bash <words>`. An `allow` command rule widens only a call that step 5 left at `ask` with reason `no rule` or a `bash` `ask`, and only when the whole folded text is one simple command (no separator above, nothing 7b or 7c would ask about, no install of section 6) whose first words match. It never turns a `deny` into anything, never beats a `chat` or `harness` rule of higher rank, and never skips steps 2, 6, 7a, 7b, 7c or section 6. Under full access (case 83) an `ask` is `deny`, as always.
+**How a deny or an ask matches (narrowing).** Only shell tools. The folded input (step 7a's text), as written and with every `\` removed, is split into simple commands at `;`, `&`, `|`, `(`, `)`, line breaks and backticks (`&&`, `||` and `$(` included), quotes joining words as the shell does (lane X's `commands`). In each, leading assignments are dropped; a wrapper of lane X's list (`sudo`, `doas`, `env`, `command`, `exec`, `builtin`, `nohup`, `time`, `stdbuf`, `chronic`, `unbuffer`, `nice`, `ionice`, `chrt`, `taskset`, `timeout`, `xargs`, `corepack`) is skipped up to the first later word that names a rule's first word, a wrapper or a shell; a shell's `-c` string, `eval`'s words and `env -S`'s string are read as text of their own (four levels at most). A command's first word counts by its program name (`/usr/bin/git` is `git`). A rule meets a command when the first words are equal and each later plain word of the rule follows in order, past flags and one value after a flag (`git -C repo push` meets `["git", "push"]`; `git commit -m "push x"` does not). A flag word of the rule meets that flag anywhere after the plain words before it, as `--force` or `--force=...`, and a one-letter flag also inside a cluster (`-f` meets `-fu`). Reading more than the shell runs only narrows more.
 
-**Fixtures.** Lane W adds cases from case 85 on to `fixtures/policy/cases.json` and rules to `policy.json`, run by all three matchers, with a case per rule kind above and one for each refused shape (a `command` on a non-shell tool, a word with a capital or a `$`).
+**What a deny or an ask does.** A matching deny makes the call `deny` at once, before steps 3 to 5, reason `rule bash <words>`. A matching ask turns an `allow` into `ask` with that reason, and stops an allow command rule from widening.
+
+**What an allow does (widening).** An `allow` command rule widens only a call that step 5 left at `ask` with reason `no rule`, or at a `bash` `ask` whose rank (chat, harness, tool, as step 5) is no higher than the command rule's, and only when the whole folded text is one plain simple command (none of `;&|()<>`, a backtick, a line break or `#`) whose first words are the rule's, in order and exactly: no wrapper, no path, no assignment before it. It never widens an install of section 6, never turns a `deny` into anything, never beats a `chat` or `harness` rule of higher rank, and never skips steps 2, 6, 7a, 7b, 7c or section 6: the widened call passes them as an allow would, or the ask stands with its own reason. A `bash` ask that narrows a shell under another name (step 3a) counts as the `bash` ask it may widen. Under full access (case 83) an `ask` is `deny`, as always. Of several matching allows, the one with the most words names the reason.
+
+**Fixtures.** `fixtures/policy/policy.json` gains six command rules (two asks and denies on `git push`, a deny on `gh repo delete`, allows on `git status`, `gh pr view` for Codex, `npm install`); `cases.json` gains cases 85 to 106, run by all three matchers; `invalid.json` holds the ten refused shapes.
+
+**Known limits** (lane W; the gate guards against a mistake, not an agent, case 89): a command reached through a script, a Makefile target, an alias, a function, `find -exec`, `ssh` or `watch` is not read (as installs reached indirectly, case 132); a force push by refspec (`git push origin +main`) has no flag word to meet; a flag's value that happens to equal a later rule word can satisfy it (`git -C push status` meets `["git", "status"]`, which only matters for narrowing).
